# Matter pages: family law and immigration. No em dashes.
# IMMIGRATION: Leaflegal must never give immigration advice. Describe only what a regulated adviser can help with and how the
# introduction works. No eligibility rules, requirements, routes, fees or timescales.
from matters_property import m

FAMILY = [
    m('divorce', 'Divorce', 'Help with ending a marriage, from application to final order.',
      ['Since April 2022, divorce in England and Wales has been “no fault”. One or both spouses apply, saying the marriage has broken down, and nobody has to prove the other was to blame. There is a minimum period of 20 weeks between applying and the conditional order, and a further six weeks before the final order.',
       'The divorce itself ends the marriage, but it doesn’t deal with money, property or children. A solicitor can handle the application and, more importantly, help you sort out the finances and arrangements that go with it.'],
      ['Making or responding to a divorce application', 'Applying for the conditional order and final order', 'Advising on timing, especially around financial orders', 'Dealing with finances and children alongside the divorce', 'Joint applications where you both agree'],
      ['Your marriage certificate', 'Your spouse’s address', 'A rough picture of your property, savings, pensions and debts', 'Any arrangements already agreed for children'],
      [('How long does a divorce take?', 'At least 26 weeks from application to final order because of the minimum waiting periods. Financial matters can make it longer.'),
       ('Do I need a solicitor for a divorce?', 'You can apply online yourself. Many people use a solicitor to make sure the finances are dealt with properly and the timing of the final order doesn’t cause problems.'),
       ('Can my spouse stop the divorce?', 'Under no fault divorce, a spouse can only dispute it on limited grounds, such as the court not having jurisdiction.')],
      'Divorce enquiries frequently lead to financial remedy and children work, which is where most of the fee value sits.'),
    m('financial-settlements', 'Financial settlements', 'Dividing property, savings, pensions and income when a marriage ends.',
      ['When a marriage or civil partnership ends, the money and property need to be divided. That can include the family home, savings, pensions, businesses and ongoing maintenance. Without a court order, claims can stay open even after the divorce is final.',
       'Many couples reach agreement through negotiation or mediation. Where they can’t, either can apply to court. A family solicitor will explain the factors the court considers and help you reach a fair outcome.'],
      ['Gathering financial disclosure', 'Negotiating a settlement', 'Pension sharing and valuations', 'Dealing with the family home and businesses', 'Court proceedings where agreement isn’t possible'],
      ['A list of property, savings, pensions and debts', 'Rough income for both of you', 'Your divorce stage and any court papers', 'Any agreement already discussed'],
      [('Is a 50:50 split automatic?', 'No. The court looks at a range of factors, including needs, resources and contributions. Equal division is often a starting point, not a rule.'),
       ('Do we need a court order if we agree?', 'It is strongly recommended. A consent order makes the agreement legally binding and can close off future claims.'),
       ('Are pensions included?', 'Yes. Pensions can be shared, offset against other assets or dealt with in other ways.')],
      'Financial settlement enquiries are among the highest value private family work, and often run for months.'),
    m('consent-orders', 'Consent orders', 'Making a financial agreement legally binding.',
      ['If you and your former partner have agreed how to divide your finances, a consent order turns that agreement into a court order. It is drafted by a solicitor, signed by both of you and approved by a judge.',
       'Without one, financial claims can remain open, sometimes for years after the divorce. A solicitor will make sure the order says what you agreed and that it will be approved.'],
      ['Drafting the consent order', 'Preparing the statement of information for the court', 'Pension sharing annexes', 'Making sure the order is fair enough to be approved', 'Submitting it to the court'],
      ['Details of what you have agreed', 'A summary of property, savings, pensions and debts', 'Your divorce case number', 'Your former partner’s contact details'],
      [('When can a consent order be made?', 'Once the conditional order in your divorce has been made. Your solicitor will advise on timing.'),
       ('Will a judge always approve it?', 'Not always. A judge checks that it is broadly fair. A well drafted order with full information is more likely to be approved.'),
       ('Can one solicitor act for both of us?', 'Usually not. One solicitor drafts the order, and the other person may take separate advice.')],
      'Consent order enquiries are well defined, often fixed fee, and quick to convert.'),
    m('child-arrangements', 'Child arrangements', 'Where children live and how they spend time with each parent.',
      ['When parents separate, they need to agree where the children will live and how much time they will spend with each parent. Most families manage this without going to court, sometimes with the help of mediation.',
       'Where agreement isn’t possible, a parent can apply for a child arrangements order, usually after attending a mediation information meeting. A family solicitor will explain the process and help you put forward arrangements that work for your children.'],
      ['Negotiating arrangements with the other parent', 'Referral to mediation', 'Applying for or responding to a child arrangements order', 'Specific issue and prohibited steps orders', 'Relocation and holidays abroad'],
      ['Your children’s ages and current arrangements', 'What you would like to happen', 'Any previous court orders', 'Any safety concerns'],
      [('Do I have to try mediation first?', 'Usually you must attend a mediation information meeting before applying to court, unless an exemption applies.'),
       ('What does the court focus on?', 'The child’s welfare is the court’s main concern. It will consider a range of factors, including the child’s wishes and needs.'),
       ('Can I take my child abroad?', 'If there is a child arrangements order saying the child lives with you, you can usually take them abroad for a short period. Otherwise you may need consent or a court order.')],
      'Children enquiries are urgent and personal. Clients usually want to speak to someone the same day.'),
    m('separation-agreements', 'Separation agreements', 'Agreeing arrangements when you separate but don’t divorce yet.',
      ['Some couples separate without divorcing straight away. A separation agreement records how you will deal with the home, money and children in the meantime.',
       'It isn’t automatically binding in the way a court order is, but a court is likely to take it into account if it was fairly made with full information and advice.'],
      ['Drafting a separation agreement', 'Financial disclosure', 'Arrangements for the family home and bills', 'Arrangements for children', 'Converting the agreement into a consent order later'],
      ['A summary of your finances', 'What you have agreed so far', 'Your plans for divorce, if any'],
      [('Is a separation agreement legally binding?', 'Not in the same way as a court order, but it can carry significant weight if made properly.'),
       ('Do we both need solicitors?', 'Taking separate advice makes the agreement more likely to be upheld.'),
       ('Can we divorce later?', 'Yes. The agreement can form the basis of a consent order when you do.')],
      'Separation agreements are a natural first instruction that often leads to a divorce and consent order.'),
    m('cohabitation-disputes', 'Cohabitation disputes', 'Property and money disputes between unmarried couples.',
      ['Unmarried couples don’t have the same rights as married couples when they separate, however long they have been together. Disputes about a shared home are usually decided under property and trust law, which looks at ownership and what was agreed or intended.',
       'Claims for children, such as housing or lump sums, may also be possible. A solicitor will explain where you stand and how to resolve it.'],
      ['Disputes about a jointly or solely owned home', 'Claims based on contributions or promises', 'Claims for the benefit of children', 'Cohabitation agreements', 'Court proceedings where needed'],
      ['How the home is owned', 'What each of you contributed', 'Any written agreement or declaration of trust', 'Details of any children'],
      [('Is there such a thing as a common law spouse?', 'No. Living together doesn’t give the same rights as marriage in England and Wales.'),
       ('Can I claim a share of a house in my partner’s name?', 'Possibly, depending on contributions and what was agreed or intended. A solicitor can assess your position.'),
       ('Should we have a cohabitation agreement?', 'It can avoid disputes later by recording who owns what and what happens if you separate.')],
      'Cohabitation disputes involve property law as well as family law, and tend to be contested and well funded.'),
    m('prenuptial-agreements', 'Prenuptial agreements', 'Agreeing how finances would be dealt with before you marry.',
      ['A prenuptial agreement sets out how you would divide your assets if the marriage ended. They are not automatically binding in England and Wales, but the courts will usually give effect to one that was freely entered into, with full understanding, and is fair.',
       'Getting it right means both of you giving full financial disclosure, taking independent advice and signing well before the wedding.'],
      ['Drafting a prenuptial or postnuptial agreement', 'Financial disclosure', 'Protecting family wealth, businesses or inheritances', 'Reviewing an agreement prepared by the other side', 'Updating an agreement after children or other changes'],
      ['Your wedding date', 'A summary of each person’s assets', 'What you want the agreement to protect'],
      [('Are prenups legally binding?', 'Not automatically, but a court will usually uphold one that was properly made and is fair.'),
       ('How far before the wedding should we sign?', 'Well in advance. Signing close to the wedding can suggest pressure.'),
       ('Can we make one after we marry?', 'Yes. This is a postnuptial agreement and works in a similar way.')],
      'Prenup enquiries come from clients with assets to protect and a fixed deadline.'),
    m('civil-partnership-dissolution', 'Ending a civil partnership', 'Dissolution, finances and children for civil partners.',
      ['Ending a civil partnership is called dissolution. It follows the same no fault process as divorce, with the same minimum waiting periods.',
       'Financial and children matters are dealt with in the same way as for married couples. A family solicitor can handle the dissolution and help you agree the rest.'],
      ['Making or responding to a dissolution application', 'Conditional and final orders', 'Financial settlements and consent orders', 'Arrangements for children', 'Joint applications'],
      ['Your civil partnership certificate', 'Your partner’s address', 'A summary of your finances'],
      [('Is dissolution the same as divorce?', 'The process is very similar, and both are no fault.'),
       ('Are finances treated differently?', 'No. Civil partners have the same financial claims as spouses.'),
       ('How long does it take?', 'At least 26 weeks from application to final order, longer if finances need resolving.')],
      'Dissolution enquiries lead to the same financial and children work as divorce.'),
]

